+/// WHICH RELATION a signature is being built for — the arc6 pruning
+/// fix (`PROGRAM_SYNTHESIS_ARC6_PRUNING_FIX.md`).
+///
+/// **Pruning-equivalence and semantic-equality are DIFFERENT RELATIONS**
+/// and this engine had merged them into one signature. The value layer
+/// (2026-08-02) correctly made `Value::List` and `Value::Tuple` distinct
+/// under semantic equality — and, because there was only ONE signature,
+/// that ALSO made them distinct for observational-equivalence pruning,
+/// which is not required and is ruinously expensive: measured on
+/// `tmpl:item_in_listvar`'s own spec, `distinct` went 1,434 -> 16,148 and
+/// a `solved` search became `halt_reason:"budget"`.
+///
+/// - `Exact` — the SEMANTIC relation. A list is not a tuple. Used for the
+///   spec-target check (`matches_target`) and for `exclude` refutations,
+///   so the value layer's gain is untouched: a tuple-expected spec is
+///   still NOT solved by a list-producing candidate.
+/// - `Coarse` — the PRUNING relation. Sequence CONTAINER type is ignored
+///   (a list and a tuple with equal elements are one behaviour class), so
+///   the equivalence pool is exactly as tight as it was before the value
+///   layer. Used only for `seen`.
+///
+/// The two are kept honest by ORDER: `register_kind` decides `solved` on
+/// the Exact signature BEFORE consulting the Coarse `seen` set, so a
+/// candidate that genuinely satisfies the spec can never be hidden by a
+/// pruning twin of the other container type. The residual price (the same
+/// price the pre-value-layer engine paid) is that only the FIRST member of
+/// a coarse class is retained as a composable building block.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+enum SigRel {
+    Exact,
+    Coarse,
+}
